# Vendored QBE

This directory contains the QBE compiler backend source, vendored so that
`tiq build --backend qbe` builds reproducibly offline and in CI.

## Upstream

- Project: https://c9x.me/compile/
- Source: https://git.sr.ht/~sircmpwn/qbe
- Version: commit `5b339287a25af5399d7c157c72a55b67532df102` (thread-local storage)
- License: ISC (see `LICENSE`)

## Local patch

The vendored tree carries two local modifications on top of upstream:

- `arm64/emit.c`: use `@PAGE`/`@PAGEOFF` relocation modifiers for `adrp`/`add`
  when targeting Mach-O (`gassym[0] == '_'`), so QBE output links correctly on
  macOS arm64. Upstream assumes ELF only.
- `Makefile`: the Darwin branch of the `config.h` rule now selects the backend
  target from `uname -m` (arm64 vs. amd64) instead of hardcoding
  `T_amd64_sysv`. `tiq`'s integrated assembler dispatches on the host CPU
  (`src/main.c`), so QBE must emit matching assembly on Apple Silicon.

## Regeneration

Build artifacts are never committed. `config.h` is generated by QBE's own
Makefile from `uname` at build time.

To refresh from upstream:

```sh
git clone https://git.sr.ht/~sircmpwn/qbe third_party/qbe
git -C third_party/qbe checkout 5b339287a25af5399d7c157c72a55b67532df102
# re-apply the arm64/emit.c Mach-O patch, then:
rm -rf third_party/qbe/.git
find third_party/qbe -type f \( -name '*.o' -name 'qbe' -name 'mcc' -name '*.d' \
  -name 'yacc.c' -name 'yacc.h' -name 'config.h' \) -delete
```
